From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: rhodep: describe the NFC controller

The part is a Samsung S3FWRN5 and mainline has a driver for it, which the
config already builds. What was missing is the node.

It is the right driver on evidence rather than on the name: the binding's
own example is s3fwrn5@27, the same address the vendor uses; the power-on
sequences match value for value and delay for delay, enable high, wake low,
20 ms, enable low, 20 ms; the vendor aliases wake to firm on a single pin
(pdata->wake = pdata->firm), which is exactly what mainline's one
wake-gpios does; the vendor's build forces CONFIG_SEC_NFC_PRODUCT_N5 and
its Kconfig spells N5 as "RN5/RNx"; and the part speaks NCI, since the
vendor hardcodes 2F 30 01 00, a well formed proprietary NCI command.

Polarity lines up too. The vendor's SEC_NFC_PW_ON is 0 -- VEN is active low
-- and s3fwrn5_phy_power_ctrl() drives gpio_en to 1 to disable and 0 to
enable, so declaring the line ACTIVE_HIGH makes the logical values match
the vendor's raw ones.

clk_req is deliberately left out: mainline has no property for it, the
vendor compiles its own clock handling out (CONFIG_SEC_NFC_GPIO_CLK is
never defined by any of its build files), and no board in the vendor tree
declares a clocks property for NFC at all.

The pinctrl state is written here because the vendor's is never applied --
it names its states nfc_active and nfc_suspend and the driver never calls
pinctrl_select_state, so those pins are left however the bootloader set
them.

**This is not known to work.** The controller does not ACK at 0x27 with VEN
asserted by hand and L9 measured at 1.8 V, in normal and in bootloader
mode, and it never asserts clk_req, which is what a live part does to ask
for its clock. What powers it has not been found: no board in the vendor
tree declares an NFC supply, and there is no load switch or NFC-named rail
anywhere in it.

It is added anyway because a failed probe costs nothing, and it removes the
one thing hand-driven GPIOs cannot rule out about themselves -- that they
were driven wrongly. If the driver's own sequence gets an ACK, the problem
was the testing; if it does not, the problem is upstream of software.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
@@ -530,6 +530,51 @@
 	pinctrl-0 = <&qup_i2c7_default>;
 	status = "okay";
 
+	/*
+	 * Samsung NFC controller. The vendor calls it sec-nfc and drives it with
+	 * its own out-of-tree driver, but the part is an S3FWRN5 and mainline
+	 * already has one:
+	 *
+	 *   - the binding's own example is s3fwrn5@27, the same address;
+	 *   - the power-on sequences match value for value and delay for delay,
+	 *     enable high, wake low, 20 ms, enable low, 20 ms;
+	 *   - the vendor aliases wake to firm on one pin (pdata->wake =
+	 *     pdata->firm), which is what mainline's single wake-gpios is;
+	 *   - the vendor's build forces CONFIG_SEC_NFC_PRODUCT_N5, and its own
+	 *     Kconfig spells N5 out as "RN5/RNx" -- S3FWRN5;
+	 *   - and the part speaks NCI: the vendor hardcodes 2F 30 01 00.
+	 *
+	 * Polarity: the vendor's SEC_NFC_PW_ON is 0, so VEN is active low, and
+	 * mainline agrees -- s3fwrn5_phy_power_ctrl() drives gpio_en to 1 to
+	 * disable and 0 to enable. Declaring it ACTIVE_HIGH is therefore right:
+	 * the logical values line up with the vendor's raw ones.
+	 *
+	 * clk_req (tlmm 7) is deliberately not described. mainline has no
+	 * property for it, the vendor's build compiles its clock handling out
+	 * entirely (CONFIG_SEC_NFC_GPIO_CLK is never defined), and no board in
+	 * the vendor tree declares a clocks property for NFC at all.
+	 *
+	 * This has not been seen to work: the part does not ACK at 0x27 with
+	 * VEN asserted by hand and L9 at 1.8 V, and it never asserts clk_req,
+	 * which is what a live controller does to ask for its clock. It is added
+	 * anyway because a failed probe costs nothing and rules out the GPIOs
+	 * being driven wrongly by hand, which is the one thing hand-testing
+	 * cannot rule out about itself.
+	 */
+	nfc@27 {
+		compatible = "samsung,s3fwrn5-i2c";
+		reg = <0x27>;
+
+		en-gpios = <&tlmm 48 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		wake-gpios = <&tlmm 8 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+
+		interrupt-parent = <&tlmm>;
+		interrupts = <9 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING>;
+
+		pinctrl-names = "default";
+		pinctrl-0 = <&nfc_default>;
+	};
+
 	cam_pmic: regulator@35 {
 		compatible = "onnn,fan53870";
 		reg = <0x35>;
@@ -1690,6 +1735,37 @@
 			function = "gpio";
 			drive-strength = <2>;
 			bias-disable;
+		};
+	};
+
+	nfc_default: nfc-default-state {
+		/*
+		 * The vendor splits these into three states it never applies --
+		 * its driver names them nfc_active/nfc_suspend and never calls
+		 * pinctrl_select_state -- so the pins are left as the bootloader
+		 * set them. Describe them properly here instead.
+		 */
+		irq-pins {
+			pins = "gpio9";
+			function = "gpio";
+			drive-strength = <2>;
+			bias-pull-down;
+			input-enable;
+		};
+
+		clk-req-pins {
+			pins = "gpio7";
+			function = "gpio";
+			drive-strength = <2>;
+			bias-pull-down;
+			input-enable;
+		};
+
+		enable-pins {
+			pins = "gpio8", "gpio48";
+			function = "gpio";
+			drive-strength = <2>;
+			bias-disable;
 		};
 	};
 
